#: includes/Core/Onboarding/StarterPack.php:779
msgid "Security hardening and cleanup"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Onboarding/StarterPack.php:780
msgid "Switches off comments, the file editor, XML-RPC and RSS feeds (feed addresses answer 403, the feed links leave the page head), hides the WordPress version, sends security headers and the Permissions-Policy header (camera, microphone, location, payment and USB off), redirects http:// to https:// when the site already runs on https (checked after saving; an endless redirect is taken back), blocks wp-config.php (and its backup copies), readme.html, license.txt and wp-config-sample.php in .htaccess, switches off directory listing, stops ?author=N and the public users list in the REST API (logged-in users keep it; the core users sitemap is not touched), replaces user names in author page addresses with a code (old /author/name/ addresses then give 404), limits login attempts, locks out an IP that tries a reserved username such as admin, hides which part of a failed login was wrong and switches off Application Passwords (new ones cannot be created; an MCP or REST client that needs them: switch off \"Harden the login page\" in Security > Login), slows down Heartbeat, keeps 10 revisions, removes the emoji script and deletes unused default themes that WordPress itself brought (Twenty*, never a manually installed one, the newest one stays as the fallback). Left off on purpose, switch them on yourself when you are sure: the canonical URL redirect and HSTS (a browser remembers HSTS for a year). Open \"What will be set\" for what each option does, what can go wrong and when to switch it off."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Onboarding/StarterPack.php:841
msgid "CAPTCHA without keys"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Onboarding/StarterPack.php:842
msgid "Login, registration, lost password and comments ask the browser of the visitor to solve a small puzzle by itself (a fraction of a second, no account, no keys, no cookies, nothing sent to a third party). A visitor without JavaScript answers a simple sum instead, so nobody is locked out. It stops mass scripted attempts; it does not tell a person from a determined script. Keys from the central application or your own Turnstile keys are used instead as soon as they exist."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Onboarding/StarterPack.php:853
msgid "The Shield stops mass scripted attempts, not a determined attacker with a computer (that one only needs a little more time). It does not replace the login attempt limit."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Onboarding/StarterPack.php:869
